1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,672
A render farm is the most expensive machine in a studio, and nobody watches it in real time.

2
00:00:06,672 --> 00:00:13,344
When a job hangs overnight on eight GPUs, the cost isn't the compute.

3
00:00:13,344 --> 00:00:20,016
It's the two shots that miss the 9am review — zero recoverable by freeing slots, forty-two GPU slots held by zombie and idle-queue jobs.

4
00:00:20,016 --> 00:00:26,328
This is 243.8 million rows of frame-level telemetry in ClickHouse.

5
00:00:26,328 --> 00:00:32,640
198 thousand jobs, 240 hosts, and it's still being written to while we watch.

6
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,758
Three Gemini agents on Google Cloud ADK.

7
00:00:37,758 --> 00:00:42,876
The Sentinel isn't given queries — it's given the schema and a goal, and it writes its own SQL through the official ClickHouse MCP server.

8
00:00:42,876 --> 00:00:47,994
You're watching it compose those against 243.8 million rows, live.

9
00:00:47,994 --> 00:00:53,112
None of them are in our repo.

10
00:00:53,112 --> 00:00:59,694
When it finds an out-of-memory failure it runs an ASOF join — the last telemetry sample before the job died.

11
00:00:59,694 --> 00:01:06,276
This one died five seconds after the host hit 97% VRAM.

12
00:01:06,276 --> 00:01:12,858
2.1 million rows touched, and the panel tells you exactly what it cost.

13
00:01:12,858 --> 00:01:19,440
Almost nothing else lets you ask a database for the row just before a moment in a single join — anywhere else that's a window function or a correlated subquery.

14
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:25,296
Error text is embedded with Vertex AI and searched by cosine distance in ClickHouse.

15
00:01:25,296 --> 00:01:31,152
It matches meaning, not keywords — so it finds the ticket from March and the fix that closed it.

16
00:01:31,152 --> 00:01:36,984
The Orchestrator weighs findings against the dailies schedule.

17
00:01:36,984 --> 00:01:42,816
A zombie on a show with a review in five hours beats a bigger overrun due tomorrow.

18
00:01:42,816 --> 00:01:48,648
And when a proposal claims it protects a shot, that claim is checked against the delivery board before the row is written — a shot that's actually on track gets the claim dropped, not recorded.

19
00:01:48,648 --> 00:01:54,480
The agent doesn't get to mark its own homework.

20
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:59,112
Nothing has touched a render host.

21
00:01:59,112 --> 00:02:03,744
A proposal is a record, and the number only moves when a human approves it — because an agent finding waste doesn't reduce your bill.

22
00:02:03,744 --> 00:02:08,376
A supervisor approving the fix does.

23
00:02:08,376 --> 00:02:15,876
Gemini, Google Cloud ADK, ClickHouse through MCP.

24
00:02:15,876 --> 00:02:23,376
This run cost about two cents and found five thousand seven hundred forty-five dollars of capacity that's burning right now — and the reviews it was about to cost.
